<title>FRANCE: EU seen valuing late-Nov French wheat at $150.17.</title>
<headline>EU seen valuing late-Nov French wheat at $150.17.</headline>
<dateline>PARIS 1996-10-31</dateline>
<text>
<p>A European Union award of 233,700 tonnes of free-market soft wheat at a maximum refund of 19.98 Ecus per tonne, reported by grain officials, values a tonne of late-November French wheat at $150.17 per tonne, Reuters calculates.</p>
<p>The figure, which should be regarded as an estimate only, is based on benchmark standard-quality French milling wheat at Rouen and may vary significantly for other EU origins and types.  </p>
<p>The calculation uses the following assumptions gathered from market sources and Reuters data.</p>
<p>    Rouen-delivered soft wheat (Nov)   850.00 francs/tonne</p>
<p>    Carrying charge (2nd-half Nov)	29.09 francs/tonne</p>
<p>    Fob charges/other charges	     27.50 francs/tonne</p>
<p>    EU refund				   19.98 ecus/tonne</p>
<p>     + EU increment (added to refund)    1.10 ecus/tonne</p>
<p>= Subsidy (at 6.61023 green rate) 139.34 francs/tonne</p>
<p>    Spot dollar/franc			5.1090 francs/dollar</p>
<p>    Implied fob price in dollars	 150.17 dollars/tonne.</p>
<p>The same formula suggests an unsubsidised French fob price of $177.45 per tonne.</p>
<p>-- Paris newsroom +331 4221 5146</p>
</text>
